{% extends 'layout.twig' %}

{% block body %}
  <div class="card help-page">
    <h2>Help</h2>
    <p class="lede">What each part of OSMHelper does, and how to use it. Most tools fetch fresh data from Online Scout Manager when you open them, so some take a moment. The Member list and Group numbers wait until you choose sections and load.</p>
    <p class="muted">What’s planned and what changed recently: see the <a href="/roadmap/">Roadmap</a>. It’s also linked at the bottom of every page.</p>

    <section class="help-section" id="home">
      <h3>Home</h3>
      <p>After you sign in, Home (nav label for the signed-in hub at <code>/dashboard/</code>) shows your name and group, and shortcuts into the tools. The public home page at <code>/</code> explains the product and lets you connect with OSM.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="dashboard">
      <h3>Group numbers</h3>
      <p class="muted">Under <strong>People → Group numbers</strong>.</p>
      <p>Tick the sections you need, then load the numbers. The ticks are the sections from when you signed in to OSM, so opening the page does not fetch counts. Explorers, Adults, waiting lists, and any section switched off under Settings → Section capacity &amp; visibility are left out. OSMHelper only asks OSM for the sections you ticked, plus the waiting list so the age bands can be filled in. The charts and tables stay until you load a different set or sign out. If a section is missing, sign out and connect again.</p>
      <p>Membership counts by section type (Beavers, Cubs, Scouts, and Squirrels when anyone of that age is on the waiting list). Shows leaders, capacity, spaces, and waiting subtotals. The header reconciles waiting-list size with <strong>Waiting of age</strong>, <strong>Squirrels</strong>, <strong>Too young</strong>, and <strong>Explorers / too old</strong>.</p>
      <p>At the top of the results, snapshot charts (same data as the tables, no extra OSM calls) show <strong>members vs spaces</strong> by section (including over capacity), <strong>waiting by age band</strong>, and <strong>fullness %</strong> by section type (sections without a set capacity are omitted). Labelled as of that load.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="members">
      <h3>Member list</h3>
      <p>Under <strong>People → Member list</strong>. Tick the sections you need, then load the list. The ticks are the sections from when you signed in to OSM, so opening the page does not fetch members. OSMHelper only asks OSM for the sections you ticked. Age and date of birth are filled in where OSM needs an extra lookup. After the list loads, the section checkboxes above the table show or hide rows without another fetch. The table is sortable, searchable, and exportable (CSV / Excel / PDF). Leaders are not listed here. Each row has a <strong>Nights</strong> link into Nights away for that member. The list stays until you load again or sign out. If a section is missing, sign out and connect again.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="find-member">
      <h3>Find a member</h3>
      <p>Under <strong>People → Find a member</strong>. Find who a postcode, phone number or parent belongs to. First tick the sections and choose <strong>Load contacts</strong>. That uses one OSM request per section and holds a copy of those contact details temporarily in your session, only on our server, for up to about 2 hours. It is cleared when it expires or when you sign out, after which you load contacts again. Then search as often as you like: searching never calls OSM. It looks at the member’s own contact details, Primary contacts 1 and 2 and the Emergency contact: postcodes, phone numbers and contact names. Your search is sent privately, so what you type is not saved in the web address, your browser history or our server logs, and it is forgotten after 2 hours or when you sign out. Part of a value is enough. Postcodes ignore spaces and capitals. Phone numbers ignore spaces, dashes and brackets, and +44, 0044 and a leading 0 count as the same. Each result shows the member, their section, and which contact and field matched, with a <strong>Nights</strong> link into Nights away. Refresh is limited to once every 5 minutes, and stops straight away if OSM returns an error, an unexpected reply, or your allowance runs low. If a load fails before any section is read, your earlier contacts are kept. Leaders in a Leaders patrol are left out, as on the Member list.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="events">
      <h3>Events</h3>
      <p>Under <strong>Badges → Events</strong>: list every event in one section by start date, oldest first, including events that have been archived in OSM. Events are marked <strong>Archived</strong> when OSM’s own archived flag on that event says so, or, if OSM sends no flag, when the event only came back from the archived request. Choose the section, then <strong>Load events</strong>. That uses two OSM requests for the section (its events, then a second request that asks OSM to include archived events), never one per event, and holds the list in your session for up to about 2 hours, or until you sign out.</p>
      <p>Pick <strong>All time</strong>, or enter <strong>From</strong> and <strong>to</strong> dates as dd/mm/yyyy (both days included; leave one blank for “from” or “until” only), then <strong>Show events</strong>. Changing the dates does not call OSM. The table can be sorted, searched, printed and exported like other tables. <strong>Download CSV</strong> gives a file with Section, Start date (dd/mm/yyyy), Event name and Archived (Yes/No) for the dates you chose. It is made from the loaded list, so it never calls OSM; if nothing is loaded yet it asks you to load first.</p>
      <p>Events needs OSM’s permission to read events, which OSMHelper started asking for on 22 Oct 2026. If you signed in before then, or the page says OSM hasn’t given permission, <a href="/logout/">sign out</a>, sign in again, and allow Events when OSM asks. OSMHelper checks this before calling OSM, so nothing is used from your allowance.</p>
      <p><strong>Refresh events</strong> can be used once every 5 minutes per section. Loading stops straight away if OSM returns an error, an unexpected reply, says too many requests (with how long to wait), or your allowance runs low. If a refresh fails, the earlier list is kept.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="checks">
      <h3>Member checks</h3>
      <p>Under <strong>People → Member checks</strong>. Troubleshooting tables: youth in more than one section (excluding Leaders and Young Leaders patrols), Young Leaders checks, and Leaders checks. Status cells use green for OK and amber for issues. Each table supports sort, search and export. Member checks still reads every section on this login, because a young person in two sections only shows up when both are loaded.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="young-leaders">
      <h3>Young Leaders</h3>
      <p>Under <strong>People → Young Leaders</strong>: see which other sections each member of a section helps in, for example which Beavers, Cubs or Scouts sections your Young Leaders support. Choose a section from the ones on your sign-in (sections with “Young Leader” or “YLU” in their name are listed first). Choosing a section does not call OSM.</p>
      <p><strong>Load members</strong> reads the section’s member list once, then each member’s record once, with a pause of at least a second between requests and a progress bar, so a section of 20 uses about 21 OSM requests. Sections of more than 40 ask you to confirm first, and sections of more than 150 are not loaded. Loading stops straight away, keeping what has loaded, if your allowance gets low, if OSM says too many requests, or at the first error. <strong>Continue loading</strong> carries on from where it stopped. You can also press <strong>Stop</strong> at any time.</p>
      <p>The table shows each member’s name, age and other sections. Search covers names and section names, <strong>Helping in</strong> shows only the members in one section, and the table can be sorted and exported (CSV / Excel / PDF) with the same three columns. None of this calls OSM. OSMHelper keeps only names, ages and section names (no contact or medical details) on our server for up to 24 hours while you stay signed in, tied to your OSM login, so going back to the page doesn’t use your allowance either. That copy is deleted when you sign out. <strong>Refresh from OSM</strong> reloads everyone and can be used once every 5 minutes per section. Leaders in the Leaders patrol are left out.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="nights-away">
      <h3>Nights away</h3>
      <p>Under <strong>Badges → Nights away</strong>: pick one or more youth members (Leaders and Young Leaders / YL patrols are omitted) and list the camps OSM has on the <strong>Nights Away staged badge</strong> (badge 94). Tick camps in the results to multi-select records. We read that badge in every Beavers / Cubs / Scouts / Explorers section this login can see, then call OSM’s <code>/v3/badges/records/other-sections</code> so camps from a previous section still appear after they move up. Each Member list row also has a <strong>Nights</strong> link.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="waiting">
      <h3>Waiting list</h3>
      <p>This page reads your group’s waiting list from OSM (<strong>Joining → Waiting list</strong>). If your group has more than one waiting list, choose one from the <strong>Waiting list</strong> menu at the top and select <strong>Show</strong>.</p>
      <p>Each applicant gets a score, and OSMHelper suggests which section they should join based on their age. The suggested section uses the age cut-offs in <strong>Settings</strong>, so check those match your group.</p>
      <p>The columns are:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>#</strong>: position by score, highest first.</li>
        <li><strong>Rank</strong>: your leaders’ own order, if you have set up Rank &amp; notes.</li>
        <li><strong>Age</strong> and <strong>Days waiting</strong> (days on the list).</li>
        <li><strong>Willing to help</strong>: shown once you have chosen a Willing to help field (see below).</li>
        <li><strong>Suggested section</strong>: based on age and your cut-offs.</li>
        <li><strong>Score</strong>: hover over or tap a score to see how it was worked out. See <a href="#waiting-score">How the score works</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Leaders’ notes</strong>: the Leaders’ Notes field from OSM. If you have set up Rank &amp; notes, your chosen notes field (for example “HNotes”) has its own column too.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Select a column heading to sort and use Search to filter. CSV, Excel and PDF exports include every applicant, even if you have searched. An empty list can be normal, for example if everyone has moved into a section.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="waiting-rank">
      <h3>Rank and notes</h3>
      <p>Rank and notes let your leaders agree the order in which applicants are offered a place, and keep notes about each one. You can also record whether each family is willing to help. The values are saved in OSM, on each applicant’s record, not on OSMHelper.</p>
      <p><strong>Setting up (once per waiting list)</strong></p>
      <ol>
        <li>In OSM, open the waiting list’s section settings, go to Customisable data, and add two text fields, for example “Rank” and “Notes”. If you want to use Willing to help, add a third text field for it too.</li>
        <li>In OSMHelper, go to <strong>Waiting list</strong> and choose <strong>Set up rank &amp; notes</strong> (later, <strong>Fields</strong>). Pick which OSM field holds the rank and which holds the notes, optionally pick the Willing to help field, then select <strong>Save fields</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>This choice is shared with everyone in your OSM group who uses OSMHelper. If your group has several waiting lists, set each one up separately.</p>
      <p><strong>Ranking</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rank 1 means first to be offered a place. Leave a rank blank if you haven’t decided yet.</li>
        <li>Two applicants can share a rank, and gaps (for example 1, 2, 5) are fine. Shared ranks are shown in score order.</li>
        <li><strong>Suggest ranks 1, 2, 3…</strong> fills in new ranks in the current order. It doesn’t save anything; you still need to review and save. <strong>Undo suggestion</strong> puts the boxes back.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Notes</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>The note box starts empty. The notes already in OSM are shown next to it and are never removed.</li>
        <li>When you save, your note is added at the top as <code>dd/mm/yy hh:mm - your name - "your note"</code> (UK time), with the earlier notes kept underneath.</li>
        <li>Leave the box empty to leave the notes alone. If the notes would grow past 2,000 characters, we don’t save that note and ask you to tidy the notes in OSM first, rather than cutting off old notes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Willing to help</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>If you have chosen a Willing to help field, each applicant has a choice of <strong>Keep</strong> (leave what is in OSM, shown next to it), <strong>Yes</strong>, <strong>No</strong> or <strong>Blank</strong>. We write “Yes”, “No” or nothing to OSM.</li>
        <li>It counts as yes when the field says Y, Yes, True or 1, or starts with the word “y”, “yes”, “yeah” or “yep” (for example “yes please” or “Y.”). A value with a slash, such as “Y/N” or “Yes/No”, counts as no, and so does anything else or blank.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Saving</strong></p>
      <ol>
        <li>Before you change anything, use CSV or Excel to download a copy. It includes every applicant and both the values in OSM and anything you have typed.</li>
        <li>Select <strong>Review changes</strong>. OSMHelper reads the latest values from OSM again and shows only the values that will change: what is in OSM now, and the new value. For notes, the new value is the whole notes field with your note on top.</li>
        <li>If another leader has changed a value in OSM since you opened the page, that row is unticked so you don’t overwrite their change. Tick it only if you want your value to replace theirs.</li>
        <li>Tick the box to confirm you have downloaded a copy, then select <strong>Save to OSM</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Changes are saved one at a time. If one fails, saving stops there. Anything already saved stays saved, and nothing after it is tried. We then read OSM again to check each saved value really stuck, and send it once more if it didn’t. The results page shows, for every value, whether it was sent, what OSM replied and what OSM now holds.</p>
      <p>To save, OSMHelper needs your permission to update member records in OSM. The first time you save, or if OSM refuses a save, <a href="/logout/">sign out</a> and sign in again, and allow it when OSM asks. Loading the ranking page uses your OSM allowance in the same way as the Waiting list page, and we won’t load or save while your allowance is low.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="waiting-score">
      <h3>How the score works</h3>
      <p>The score gives a quick, consistent starting point. A higher score means a stronger claim to a place. It is worked out as:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>age in years (including part-years, so 8 years 6 months counts as 8.5) × 3, plus</li>
        <li>months on the waiting list (days on the list ÷ 30), plus</li>
        <li>20 if Willing to help is yes. This only counts once a Willing to help field is chosen in <strong>Fields</strong>; until then it adds nothing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The score is shown to one decimal place. Hover over or tap a score to see the working, for example “Age 8.5 × 3 = 25.5 + 10.0 months + willing 20 = 55.5”. On the ranking page, changing Willing to help shows the effect on the score straight away (marked *) before you save. If OSM has no date of birth for an applicant, their age shows as Unknown and counts as 0. If OSMHelper can’t read an applicant’s details from OSM at all, the score shows N/A and they appear at the bottom.</p>
      <p><strong>Example (made-up numbers):</strong> an applicant aged 8 years 6 months, on the list for 300 days, who is willing to help: 8.5 × 3 = 25.5, plus 300 ÷ 30 = 10.0, plus 20 = <strong>55.5</strong>.</p>
      <p>The score is worked out in OSMHelper each time the page loads and is never saved to OSM. <strong>#</strong> is the applicant’s position by score. <strong>Rank</strong> is your leaders’ own decision and can differ from the score order, for example to keep siblings together.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="wordpress-form">
      <h3>WordPress joining form</h3>
      <p>If your group website uses WordPress, you can add a “join our waiting list” form to it. When a parent sends the form, OSMHelper adds the child straight to your OSM waiting list using <strong>your</strong> OSM login. Parents don’t need an OSM login, and your group doesn’t need to set up its own OSM app or API keys.</p>
      <p>Everything you need is under <strong>Joining → Joining form</strong> once you’re signed in: the plugin download, your site key, step-by-step setup and troubleshooting. In short:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Download the plugin from the Joining form page and install the zip in WordPress (<strong>Plugins → Add New → Upload Plugin</strong>), then activate it.</li>
        <li>On the Joining form page, choose your OSM waiting list and select Save to create a site key.</li>
        <li>In WordPress, open <strong>OSM Settings → Waiting List</strong>, enter <code>https://osmhelper.co.uk</code> as the OSM Helper base URL and paste the site key.</li>
        <li>Add <code>[osm_waiting_list]</code> to a page and publish it.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The form asks for the child’s name, date of birth and home address (address line 1, line 2, town, county, postcode), and up to two parents’ names, emails and phone numbers. Each parent can tick “Receive text messages from Leaders?”, which turns on OSM’s receive-texts setting for their phone. There’s an optional note box; the note is saved to the Notes field you chose under <a href="#waiting-rank">Rank and notes</a>, stamped “Parent (website form)”.</p>
      <p>In WordPress you can also turn on a confirmation email to the parents (with your own reply-to address), Google reCAPTCHA or Cloudflare Turnstile against spam, and an address lookup: Google address search (needs a Google key) or a free postcode check with postcodes.io.</p>
      <p>OSMHelper passes each form straight to OSM and doesn’t keep a copy. To do that, it keeps an encrypted OSM access key for the form (not your password) on the server until you turn the form off on the Joining form page.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="top-awards">
      <h3>Top awards</h3>
      <p>Under <strong>Badges → Top awards</strong>: pick one youth section (Beavers / Cubs / Scouts / Explorers), then calculate. Counts activity and staged badges since each member joined the section, then proposes Chief Scout Bronze (Beavers, /4), Silver (Cubs, /6) or Gold (Scouts, /6) progress — incomplete as <code>x n/T dd-Mon-yy</code>, complete as <code>n/T dd-Mon-yy</code> (UK date at write time; “needs update” compares the count only, not the date). Review current vs proposed, export a copy, tick saved, then confirm. Tick rows or use <strong>Write this one</strong> for selective updates. Scouts Gold uses known badge 1539 / requirement 114339; Beavers Bronze badge 1529 (progress requirement locked from OSM records); Cubs Silver still discovered until its badge id is confirmed. Writes use <code>updateSingleRecord</code> only (fail-fast). Requires <code>section:badge:write</code> — re-login after scope changes. Results cache ~2 hours; Refresh uses quota. API failures land in a redacted server log; signed-in Home shows the latest few.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="equipment">
      <h3>Equipment</h3>
      <p>Quartermaster lists and items for the section you choose under Settings → Equipment. Pick the section that holds your kit (often Adults). Shows all OSM fields (name, quantity, description, condition, broken, location, purchase date, notes, renewal price). Rows with Broken &gt; 0 are highlighted. Select multiple items and move them to a location from Settings → Equipment locations. You always review the list before Confirm; OSMHelper then updates Location (column 3) via OSM’s <code>updateListItemColumnValue</code> (value=location), spaced ~150ms apart to stay rate-limit friendly. Add an item from the top of the page: name is required, broken is 0 if you leave it blank, and location can be typed or picked from your Settings list. Review, then Confirm creates one row via OSM’s <code>saveItemRowDetails</code>. The <strong>Equipment</strong> menu has shortcuts to the list, to <strong>Add an item</strong> and to <strong>Locations</strong> (in Settings). Editing an existing item is not available yet. The table supports sort, search and export (CSV / Excel / PDF), same as the Member list, Member checks and Waiting list. If OSM blocks item detail for a list, the list names still show with a short note.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="bank">
      <h3>Bank accounts</h3>
      <p class="muted">Under <strong>Finance → Bank accounts</strong>.</p>
      <p>Lists bank accounts for the finance section you pick in Settings (active accounts by default; optional show deleted). Balances are OSM pence ÷ 100. Click an account to see transactions (date, reference, amount, type/transfer, description) via OSM’s v3 accounting API. Sort, search and export on both tables. Read-only — no bank writes.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="help-section" id="all-transfers">
      <h3>All transfers</h3>
      <p>Under Finance → All transfers: every transfer line across your active bank accounts, newest first, with the account name on each row. Unpaired legs (same date and absolute amount without a matching +/−) are highlighted. Use it when balances don’t add up. Same v3 read API; rate-limit friendly spacing between accounts.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="settings">
      <h3>Settings</h3>
      <p>Age cutoffs, capacity per section, which section Equipment uses, which section Bank accounts uses, and a named <strong>Equipment locations</strong> list (add, edit, reorder, remove). Saves on this server (not only in your browser session) so they stick after logout. Unticking <strong>Show</strong> on a section leaves it off the Group numbers picker. Locations power the Equipment bulk-move destination list. The WordPress joining form has its own page, <strong>Joining → Joining form</strong>; Settings links to it.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="privacy">
      <h3>Your data and privacy</h3>
      <p>OSM stays the record. Your OSM access token is held only in your session on our server. Most pages fetch fresh data from OSM when you open them; the waiting list pages reuse a copy for up to 5 minutes and Young Leaders for up to 24 hours (see below). Anything a page holds for you while you work (a member list, group numbers, events, or the contacts Find a member searches) stays in that session only.</p>
      <p>Every session ends 2 hours after you sign in, or when you sign out, even if you are still using it, and its file on our server is cleaned up shortly after. The top of each page shows when your session ends (for example “Signed in until 23:40”). If it ends while you are working, sign in again and you go back to the page you were on.</p>
      <p>Top awards results are kept for up to 2 hours so the page loads faster, then deleted. To save OSM requests, the waiting list pages keep a copy on our server: applicants’ names, dates of birth, dates joined, every one of your waiting list custom fields and the leaders’ notes, plus your section list. Only you can see that copy (it is tied to your OSM login), and it is deleted 5 minutes after it was fetched, whether or not you come back. It is also deleted when you sign out, and the waiting list copy is deleted for everyone when OSMHelper saves a change to that waiting list. <strong>Refresh from OSM</strong> on the waiting list pages fetches it again straight away. Young Leaders keeps only members’ names, ages and the names of the other sections they are in, for each section you load. Only you can see it, and it is deleted 24 hours after it was fetched or when you sign out, whichever comes first. Find a member searches, and the members you pick in Nights away, are sent privately and never appear in web addresses or server logs.</p>
      <p>The only things saved on our server are your group settings (age cut-offs, capacity, which sections each tool uses, kit locations and your chosen waiting list fields), saved with your OSM user number so we know whose settings are whose, and, if you use the <a href="#wordpress-form">WordPress joining form</a>, an encrypted OSM access key for that form, kept until you turn the form off. We also keep a short error log to fix problems. It records what failed, the error code and which section, never OSM’s message text, names, contact details or member numbers, and entries are deleted after 30 days.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="rate-limit">
      <h3>API rate limit</h3>
      <p>OSM applies an API allowance <strong>per user</strong> (your OSM login) — it is not a “how busy OSM is” meter. Home and the hub show your limit, remaining requests, and when the window resets. A banner appears site-wide when you are nearing the limit (and again when remaining is critical). Open fewer heavy pages until it refreshes. The Member list and Group numbers use quota only when you load the sections you ticked. Group numbers also reads the waiting list at that point. Member checks, Waiting list, Equipment, Bank accounts, Top awards and Nights away each use quota when you open them. Young Leaders uses quota only when you press Load or Refresh: one request for the member list plus one per member, paced, and nothing for 24 hours after that.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="osm-busy">
      <h3>“OSM is busy, try again in N minutes”</h3>
      <p>If OSM’s allowance runs out, OSM can lock your login out of its API. To stop that happening, OSMHelper watches the allowance OSM reports on every reply.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Running low:</strong> when fewer than 10% of your requests are left (and never fewer than 20), OSMHelper pauses page loads for your login until the allowance resets (at least 1 minute). Essential requests still go through: a save you have reviewed and confirmed, and the WordPress joining form.</li>
        <li><strong>“Too many requests” or none left:</strong> OSMHelper stops sending anything to OSM for your login at once, including saves and the WordPress joining form. It waits the longer of what OSM asks for and the time until your allowance resets: at least 1 minute, at most about 25 hours (15 minutes if OSM gives neither). It never retries on its own.</li>
        <li><strong>OSM has blocked OSMHelper:</strong> OSMHelper stops every request to OSM, for every user, including sign-in, saves and the WordPress joining form, and stays stopped until the site administrator clears the block. Continuing after a block could make it permanent.</li>
        <li><strong>Can’t save its pause record:</strong> if OSMHelper can’t save its record of the pause on our server, it stops all requests to OSM rather than risk your allowance, and tries again after 5 minutes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The pause is shared by every page, so opening other pages won’t make it worse. The WordPress joining form is also limited to 20 submissions per 15 minutes from any one internet address (normally your WordPress website’s server).</p>
      <p>While the message shows, wait the number of minutes it gives, then reload the page. Waiting list pages you opened in the last 5 minutes may still show, from the copy OSMHelper kept.</p>
      <p>The waiting list reads everyone’s waiting list fields in one bulk request, plus one request per applicant for their date of birth and date joined (from their own OSM record), up to 40 requests per page load. It keeps the result for 5 minutes, so reopening a page or switching between the list and ranking uses no extra requests. On a long list some applicants show as “not loaded yet”: reload the page within 5 minutes to load the next batch (what was already loaded is kept for 5 minutes, then deleted). <strong>Refresh from OSM</strong> ignores the kept copy and loads it again, still within the same limits.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="tables">
      <h3>Tables (sort, search, export)</h3>
      <p>On <strong>Member list</strong>, <strong>Member checks</strong>, <strong>Young Leaders</strong>, <strong>Waiting list</strong>, <strong>Equipment</strong>, <strong>Top awards</strong> and <strong>Nights away</strong>, click a column header to sort, use Search to filter rows, and export (most pages export the rows you can see; the Waiting list and ranking pages export every row) as <strong>CSV</strong>, <strong>Excel</strong> (.xls), or <strong>PDF</strong> (opens the print dialog — choose Save as PDF). Data tables use gentle alternating row colours for readability.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="roadmap">
      <h3>Roadmap</h3>
      <p>See what is planned next on the <a href="/roadmap/">Roadmap</a> page (phases P2–P4 and backlog).</p>
    </section>

    <section class="help-section" id="contact">
      <h3>Questions?</h3>
      <p>Email <a href="mailto:hello@jonbloor.co.uk">hello@jonbloor.co.uk</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
{% endblock %}
